1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:03,440
They're everywhere.

2
00:00:04,560 --> 00:00:08,360
All wanting is to dig deep into our pockets and purses and feed them.

3
00:00:08,780 --> 00:00:10,400
They can do just about anything.

4
00:00:11,360 --> 00:00:14,340
They're made of metal, plastic, and miles of wire.

5
00:00:15,360 --> 00:00:16,220
They're one of a million.

6
00:00:16,760 --> 00:00:18,420
Everybody needs to wash their clothes.

7
00:00:19,520 --> 00:00:20,920
Or one of a kind.

8
00:00:21,280 --> 00:00:23,860
This is made by a San Quentin prisoner.

9
00:00:24,560 --> 00:00:25,720
They can be addictive.

10
00:00:27,660 --> 00:00:28,380
Annoying.

11
00:00:28,381 --> 00:00:31,540
and sometimes really weird.

12
00:00:32,540 --> 00:00:37,960
Now, the untold story of Coin Operated on Modern Marvels.

13
00:00:49,720 --> 00:00:52,000
Small change is big business,

14
00:00:52,680 --> 00:00:56,680
and vending machines are the biggest source of revenue in the coin-operated industry.

15
00:00:56,681 --> 00:01:00,680
Every 15 minutes, over 3.5 million coins are inserted

16
00:01:00,681 --> 00:01:03,680
into coin-operated vending machines in the United States

17
00:01:03,681 --> 00:01:04,680
alone.

18
00:01:04,681 --> 00:01:10,680
You can buy anything, from aspirin to ice cream.

19
00:01:10,681 --> 00:01:13,680
But by a wide margin, the most common vending machines are

20
00:01:13,681 --> 00:01:17,680
those that dispense drinks and snacks.

21
00:01:17,681 --> 00:01:20,680
The food and refreshment vending industry in the United

22
00:01:20,681 --> 00:01:25,680
States is north of $40 billion a year.

23
00:01:25,681 --> 00:01:33,960
100 million people a day walk up to nearly 7 million machines in the United States and

24
00:01:33,961 --> 00:01:35,037
purchase a product.

25
00:01:35,038 --> 00:01:38,720
There are approximately a million people employed in the industry

26
00:01:38,721 --> 00:01:41,795
and over 13,500 companies providing service.

27
00:01:41,796 --> 00:01:44,660
One of the latest developments in vending

28
00:01:44,661 --> 00:01:49,160
machine technology makes the jobs of those who maintain them much more efficient.

29
00:01:49,161 --> 00:01:53,540
Here at Ace Vending we use handheld computers.

30
00:01:53,541 --> 00:01:58,280
What the driver does is open the machine, plugs into the computer board of the vending

31
00:01:58,281 --> 00:02:04,380
machine, downloads the financial and sales data, and then we collect that data, bring

32
00:02:04,381 --> 00:02:06,920
it back to the warehouse.

33
00:02:06,921 --> 00:02:10,600
Every time the drivers finish their rounds, they download the data into the company's

34
00:02:10,601 --> 00:02:14,520
computer system.

35
00:02:14,521 --> 00:02:20,080
This gives an exact accounting of the money collected and of every product sold.

36
00:02:20,081 --> 00:02:24,620
By continually updating the sales history of each machine, warehouse workers are able

37
00:02:24,621 --> 00:02:29,360
to accurately fill containers with the anticipated restocking needs of the machines on each driver's

38
00:02:29,361 --> 00:02:30,360
route.

39
00:02:30,361 --> 00:02:37,720
And what that does is prevents him from coming in twice by coming in and looking in and see

40
00:02:37,721 --> 00:02:40,900
what the vending machine needs and going back to the truck and getting it.

41
00:02:40,901 --> 00:02:43,400
already preloaded for him based on the download.

42
00:02:45,140 --> 00:02:47,820
Vending machine technology has come a long way.

43
00:02:49,780 --> 00:02:51,880
Scholars believe that the first vending machine

44
00:02:51,881 --> 00:02:54,660
was a holy water dispenser used in Greek temples

45
00:02:54,661 --> 00:02:56,980
dating back to 215 B.C.

46
00:02:58,200 --> 00:02:59,840
When deposited in the machine,

47
00:03:00,620 --> 00:03:02,220
the weight of the coin caused a lever

48
00:03:02,221 --> 00:03:04,580
to lower and dispense a small amount of the water.

49
00:03:07,500 --> 00:03:09,700
Today's vending machines can be complex.

50
00:03:09,701 --> 00:03:14,140
able to take all kinds of payments, deliver change,

51
00:03:14,240 --> 00:03:16,640
and offer an almost dizzying variety of goodies.

52
00:03:20,540 --> 00:03:24,140
At Crane Merchandising's enormous 500,000 square foot

53
00:03:24,240 --> 00:03:28,160
facility in St. Louis, snack and drink machines are born.

54
00:03:28,260 --> 00:03:32,260
The process starts with large rolls of steel sheeting.

55
00:03:32,360 --> 00:03:37,760
Each coil will manufacture about 125 to 150 machines.

56
00:03:37,761 --> 00:03:44,860
We will mount the steel, the coil steel, onto the dual mandrel, and the mandrel will take

57
00:03:44,861 --> 00:03:48,340
it over to a decoiling process.

58
00:03:48,341 --> 00:03:53,220
There's a deep impression into the floor, and we'll take the bend out, and then we sure

59
00:03:53,221 --> 00:03:55,700
it's about 15 feet in length.

60
00:03:55,701 --> 00:04:00,880
They move from there to a punching, notching station.

61
00:04:00,881 --> 00:04:05,480
From there they move into a roll farming station, and the front and the back edge of the cabinet

62
00:04:05,481 --> 00:04:06,880
are rolled on.

63
00:04:06,881 --> 00:04:10,120
And once they've left there, they move into the welding station where the components

64
00:04:10,121 --> 00:04:13,640
for the inside of the cabinet are added.

65
00:04:13,641 --> 00:04:18,380
The next stop is the tangent bending station, where the long steel sheet is bent to form

66
00:04:18,381 --> 00:04:24,200
the top and two sides of the cabinet.

67
00:04:24,201 --> 00:04:29,300
Further down the assembly line, the bottom and the legs are attached.

68
00:04:29,301 --> 00:04:33,820
Then workers hang the cabinets and various parts on a moving conveyor that carries them

69
00:04:33,821 --> 00:04:36,620
washer and dryer.

70
00:04:36,720 --> 00:04:39,120
Next, it's on to a room where they are coated with a dry,

71
00:04:39,220 --> 00:04:41,020
powdered paint.

72
00:04:41,120 --> 00:04:43,560
This line has an electrostatic charge on it,

73
00:04:43,660 --> 00:04:46,260
so that when the powder is sprayed on them,

74
00:04:46,360 --> 00:04:47,460
it is like a magnet.

75
00:04:47,560 --> 00:04:49,860
It holds it on there while they're being painted.

76
00:04:49,960 --> 00:04:52,680
That charge is held on them until they go into an oven.

77
00:04:52,780 --> 00:04:55,280
They're heated to 450 degrees.

78
00:04:55,380 --> 00:04:58,180
That melts that paint powder, becomes a liquid,

79
00:04:58,280 --> 00:05:02,380
and then no longer needs the electrostatic charge.

80
00:05:02,381 --> 00:05:08,340
Once the basic structure of each type of machine is completed, workers install all

81
00:05:08,341 --> 00:05:13,000
the internal and electronic parts, including the defining electronic coin

82
00:05:13,001 --> 00:05:17,860
mechanisms. This is the standard coin mechanism. Sits inside a vending machine

83
00:05:17,861 --> 00:05:20,980
and when coins are put in through the front of the machine, roots them through

84
00:05:20,981 --> 00:05:25,620
here and there's a series of sensors inside and it uses this to measure how

85
00:05:25,621 --> 00:05:28,220
the coin going through changes the frequency. From that it can very

86
00:05:28,221 --> 00:05:32,620
accurately determine thickness, what the diameter is, what alloy it's made out of.

87
00:05:32,621 --> 00:05:37,020
There used to be lots of problems with frauds and counterfeit coins going through.

88
00:05:37,021 --> 00:05:41,260
That more or less disappeared with the advent of this electronic recognition technology.

89
00:05:41,261 --> 00:05:47,420
And to avoid losing sales from consumers not carrying enough change, most modern

90
00:05:47,421 --> 00:05:50,300
vending machines also have a mechanism to accept paper money.

91
00:05:50,301 --> 00:05:54,877
Virtually, every vending machine has a bill validator.

92
00:05:54,878 --> 00:05:57,420
It recognizes bills by shining

93
00:05:57,421 --> 00:06:02,300
light through it, little LEDs that go through it, known points on here, and depending on

94
00:06:02,301 --> 00:06:07,180
the ink and if the paper that had been made by the mint, it'll know that it's a good bill,

95
00:06:07,181 --> 00:06:10,560
it'll know what denomination is, it also checks magnetic inks.

96
00:06:10,561 --> 00:06:15,800
Many people don't realize this, but the black ink on the front of the dollar bill is magnetic.

97
00:06:15,801 --> 00:06:19,840
When manufacturing snack machines, customer specifications determine the types of metal

98
00:06:19,841 --> 00:06:22,140
spirals to be installed on the shelves.

99
00:06:22,141 --> 00:06:26,660
This is the heart of a snack machine.

100
00:06:26,661 --> 00:06:28,240
You notice they come in different varieties.

101
00:06:28,241 --> 00:06:30,720
This is a 30 count, this is a 11 count.

102
00:06:30,721 --> 00:06:33,900
Your 11 count usually produces chips or pastry,

103
00:06:33,901 --> 00:06:38,700
and this one do candy bars and peanuts and things like that.

104
00:06:38,701 --> 00:06:40,660
The back end of each spiral is attached

105
00:06:40,661 --> 00:06:44,100
to a small 24-volt motor that rotates once

106
00:06:44,101 --> 00:06:47,120
to push the selected product forward,

107
00:06:47,121 --> 00:06:49,220
dropping it into the tray at the bottom of the machine.

108
00:06:53,040 --> 00:06:55,560
Crane merchandising has come up with an entertaining way

109
00:06:55,561 --> 00:07:00,200
dispense 20-ounce bottled soft drinks without dropping them.

110
00:07:00,201 --> 00:07:04,600
Let me show you one of our latest developments.

111
00:07:04,601 --> 00:07:10,240
The XY delivery mechanism rises to the height of the shelf and delivers the product conveniently

112
00:07:10,241 --> 00:07:11,800
to the delivery port.

113
00:07:11,801 --> 00:07:16,960
There's a good bit of theater, but most importantly, when the product is delivered, it hasn't agitated

114
00:07:16,961 --> 00:07:20,960
the drink, so when I open the cap, it doesn't explode.

115
00:07:20,961 --> 00:07:24,880
Crane has also incorporated new technology in their hot beverage machines.

116
00:07:24,881 --> 00:07:27,240
to remedy the old problem of a drink pouring directly

117
00:07:27,241 --> 00:07:31,980
down the drain if the cup fails to fall into place.

118
00:07:31,981 --> 00:07:33,880
What makes this equipment unique

119
00:07:33,881 --> 00:07:36,640
is that we have a SureVin detection mechanism.

120
00:07:36,641 --> 00:07:38,520
It's infrared detectors to determine

121
00:07:38,521 --> 00:07:41,180
whether the cup is in the station or not.

122
00:07:41,181 --> 00:07:44,480
If the cup is not detected in the cup station,

123
00:07:44,481 --> 00:07:46,840
then the electronics signal the cup turret

124
00:07:46,841 --> 00:07:49,580
to turn up to three additional times.

125
00:07:49,581 --> 00:07:52,120
And if the cup is still not delivered,

126
00:07:52,121 --> 00:07:54,220
the consumer's money is refunded.

127
00:07:55,460 --> 00:07:58,420
While Crane Merchandising's machines can be found all over the country,

128
00:07:59,560 --> 00:08:02,500
a giant vending machine called the BAM Automat

129
00:08:02,501 --> 00:08:05,160
exists only in New York City's East Village.

130
00:08:06,040 --> 00:08:07,820
Some people look at it as a vending machine,

131
00:08:07,920 --> 00:08:11,540
but we look at it more like little peep windows for foodies.

132
00:08:13,460 --> 00:08:14,560
Unseen by the public,

133
00:08:15,020 --> 00:08:16,800
the shelves are being restocked by the kitchen

134
00:08:16,801 --> 00:08:18,480
on the other side of the wall of doors.

135
00:08:18,481 --> 00:08:24,480
The way it works is you get a bunch of coins and then you just drop them into the slot.

136
00:08:26,480 --> 00:08:31,540
And then you just pull any of these doors with the food in it and you just pull the food out.

137
00:08:32,640 --> 00:08:33,340
Voila.

138
00:08:34,660 --> 00:08:37,460
Automats once thrived in both New York and Philadelphia.

139
00:08:38,280 --> 00:08:43,180
At their peak of popularity in the 1950s, there were 180 automats in the two cities.

140
00:08:43,440 --> 00:08:46,300
And they fed more than 800,000 people a day.

141
00:08:46,301 --> 00:08:52,300
Back then, they were large restaurants with dining areas, offering a full selection of

142
00:08:52,301 --> 00:08:55,300
items to put together substantial meals.

143
00:08:55,301 --> 00:09:00,300
The last of the original automats closed in 1991.

144
00:09:00,301 --> 00:09:05,300
With the explosion of fast food restaurants, they weren't the bargain they once were.

145
00:09:05,301 --> 00:09:07,300
Something Bam would like to change.

146
00:09:07,301 --> 00:09:12,300
Back then, a typical grilled cheese would be probably a nickel.

147
00:09:12,301 --> 00:09:17,140
Now it's $2, but $2 in today's value, it's worth it.

148
00:09:18,380 --> 00:09:23,220
BAM wants to reinvigorate American interest in automats with its variety of fresh foods.

149
00:09:25,100 --> 00:09:29,424
But over a century ago, all it took was a stick of gum to launch the U.S.

150
00:09:29,425 --> 00:09:31,260
coin-operated vending business.

151
00:09:32,260 --> 00:09:36,320
The first vending machines in the United States were introduced in 1888,

152
00:09:36,740 --> 00:09:40,900
tutti-frutti gum machines placed by the Thomas Adams Company on the New York subway system.

153
00:09:42,300 --> 00:09:47,780
The OK Manufacturing Company in Salt Lake City proves you can still build a thriving business on gum.

154
00:09:48,740 --> 00:09:51,800
Brothers Jeff and Kurt Osler entered the business in a big way.

155
00:09:53,240 --> 00:09:55,360
The idea in vending is to capture the quarter.

156
00:09:56,240 --> 00:09:59,420
And in order to capture the quarter, you have to capture their attention first.

157
00:09:59,421 --> 00:10:03,620
There is nothing better than the big and giant gumball machine to capture attention.

158
00:10:03,880 --> 00:10:06,480
This machine holds 40,000 pieces of gum.

159
00:10:06,481 --> 00:10:13,040
It stands about six foot ten up here holds the 40,000 gumballs which if we really create in dollars

160
00:10:13,041 --> 00:10:17,720
That's ten thousand dollars that this machine would dispense out and put in its cash box

161
00:10:17,721 --> 00:10:21,600
Our very first time we went and put one out three days after we had there had over

162
00:10:22,260 --> 00:10:26,920
$500 in it. That's when we knew we had a great concept. I used to put my child

163
00:10:27,600 --> 00:10:33,480
Into the side they would go in to collect the machine because that's where the money was and push the quarters out

164
00:10:33,481 --> 00:10:37,580
For the Auslers, the giant gumball machine was just the beginning.

165
00:10:37,581 --> 00:10:41,480
Another standout in their growing product line is the Roadrunner.

166
00:10:41,481 --> 00:10:47,480
We decided people wanted a little more entertainment, maybe a little more bang out of their quarter.

167
00:10:47,481 --> 00:10:54,480
We made a spiral machine so that after you put your quarter in and you turn the handle, you get a little bit of a show.

168
00:10:54,481 --> 00:10:58,480
In addition to getting a piece of gum.

169
00:10:58,481 --> 00:11:03,860
Here at OK Manufacturing, we make almost all of our own plastic parts.

170
00:11:03,861 --> 00:11:09,200
For instance, we start with plastic sheets that we put into our plastic vacuum thermal

171
00:11:09,201 --> 00:11:15,160
former where it's heated up and sucked over a mold which we also make here.

172
00:11:15,161 --> 00:11:19,500
We make a lot of our own circular globes here through a custom process.

173
00:11:19,501 --> 00:11:25,240
We take sheets of clear plastic, place it in an oven where it's heated up, where it

174
00:11:25,241 --> 00:11:27,040
becomes nice and pliable.

175
00:11:27,041 --> 00:11:30,280
It's placed in our custom machine,

176
00:11:30,281 --> 00:11:34,060
where we actually inject air that blows the globe up

177
00:11:34,061 --> 00:11:36,000
until it hits a micro switch.

178
00:11:36,001 --> 00:11:38,060
And that micro switch is set at a certain height

179
00:11:38,061 --> 00:11:40,480
depending on the diameter of the globe.

180
00:11:45,240 --> 00:11:47,980
OK's craftsmen cut and clean up any rough edges

181
00:11:47,981 --> 00:11:53,140
on the blobs and other plastic parts, then assemble them

182
00:11:53,141 --> 00:11:56,240
into gumball machines.

183
00:11:56,241 --> 00:11:59,440
The coin mechanism, purchased from an outside manufacturer,

184
00:11:59,441 --> 00:12:02,640
is one of the last parts to be attached.

185
00:12:02,641 --> 00:12:04,380
This is our standard coin mechanism

186
00:12:04,381 --> 00:12:06,380
that we put inside all of our equipment

187
00:12:06,381 --> 00:12:07,720
because of the quality.

188
00:12:07,721 --> 00:12:09,620
You'll go ahead, you'll put the quarter in.

189
00:12:09,621 --> 00:12:13,280
As the quarter then starts to turn here on the back side,

190
00:12:13,281 --> 00:12:14,900
you'll notice the circumference check

191
00:12:14,901 --> 00:12:16,640
to make sure that's the correct size.

192
00:12:16,641 --> 00:12:18,200
If it happens to be a penny nickel or dime,

193
00:12:18,201 --> 00:12:19,780
it will go ahead and just drop through.

194
00:12:19,781 --> 00:12:21,040
As it starts to come, you'll notice

195
00:12:21,041 --> 00:12:22,640
you have a little ratchet arm here

196
00:12:22,641 --> 00:12:25,400
is making sure that a kid can't rack it back and forth

197
00:12:25,401 --> 00:12:27,780
to try to get the extra product.

198
00:12:27,781 --> 00:12:30,880
And as you come down, the quarter will come and fall on out.

199
00:12:32,020 --> 00:12:33,820
The Osler brothers keep finding ways

200
00:12:33,821 --> 00:12:35,580
to stand out in the gumball market.

201
00:12:37,080 --> 00:12:39,660
It could be thanks to their competitive natures.

202
00:12:39,661 --> 00:12:42,620
This is our most interactive gumball machine,

203
00:12:42,621 --> 00:12:46,560
our soccer ball, which allows you to be able to play.

204
00:12:46,561 --> 00:12:48,260
Now you can play with your food.

205
00:12:48,261 --> 00:12:49,700
You're ready to go Lowe's.

206
00:12:52,640 --> 00:12:59,260
This hybrid machine that mixes sport and gumballs makes it a viable addition to another major coin-operated industry.

207
00:13:02,880 --> 00:13:04,780
Arcade games and amusements.

208
00:13:06,680 --> 00:13:16,060
For more than a century, it's been a hypnotic realm of flashing lights, crazy mechanics, and freaky visuals.

209
00:13:17,540 --> 00:13:19,960
The public's always looking for something new.

210
00:13:19,961 --> 00:13:31,960
But how those old standbys, jukeboxes and pinball machines managed to adapt and survive in the 21st century?

211
00:13:34,960 --> 00:13:46,867
According to the National Automatic Merchandising Association, in 2007, consumers worldwide purchased over 21 billion bottled beverages from vending machines, enough

212
00:13:46,868 --> 00:13:48,960
to circle the earth 70 times.

213
00:13:48,961 --> 00:13:51,840
Coin Operated will return on Modern Marvels.

214
00:13:59,120 --> 00:14:01,440
From arm wrestling a mechanical brute,

215
00:14:02,060 --> 00:14:04,820
to trying to keep up with a virtual jump rope,

216
00:14:05,380 --> 00:14:07,960
from those creepy old fortune tellers,

217
00:14:08,180 --> 00:14:10,200
to dancing cowboys,

218
00:14:11,460 --> 00:14:15,180
Coin Operated machines have been amusing and entertaining us for more than a century.

219
00:14:16,140 --> 00:14:21,200
Before radio and TV, Penny Arcades offered the public a variety of entertainment,

220
00:14:22,260 --> 00:14:24,500
including the earliest forms of moving pictures.

221
00:14:25,780 --> 00:14:29,180
This is an early motion picture machine built in the late 1800s.

222
00:14:30,420 --> 00:14:34,640
It's called a clamshell mutoscope. It's what they used before film.

223
00:14:35,220 --> 00:14:37,960
And if I open this up, I can show you the insides.

224
00:14:37,961 --> 00:14:45,720
and it's actually the coin that starts the mechanism which activates the flip cards.

225
00:14:45,721 --> 00:14:51,480
The person operating the machine is the motor and the faster you turn the handle,

226
00:14:51,481 --> 00:14:55,133
the faster the picture will go by.

227
00:14:55,134 --> 00:15:00,720
The flip cards do one complete rotation 360 degrees,

228
00:15:00,721 --> 00:15:05,400
which is approximately 1500 cards and then it shuts off.

229
00:15:05,401 --> 00:15:12,400
Mutoscopes were soon replaced with kinetoscopes, invented by Thomas Edison and William Dixon.

230
00:15:12,401 --> 00:15:19,400
The first kinetoscope parlor opened in 1894, offering the public its first chance to see motion pictures on film.

231
00:15:19,401 --> 00:15:27,400
One side of the cabinet contained a battery that powered a small motor, and on the other side, beyond a wooden panel,

232
00:15:27,401 --> 00:15:31,400
a projection lamp lit 50 feet of film that ran through the machine.

233
00:15:31,401 --> 00:15:37,160
But when Edison perfected the movie projector, he made kinetoscopes instantly obsolete.

234
00:15:37,161 --> 00:15:42,600
Coin-operated music machines also first appeared in the late 1800s.

235
00:15:42,601 --> 00:15:47,560
Some of the earliest were basically big music boxes.

236
00:15:47,561 --> 00:15:55,960
But soon, many became very elaborate technological fantasies.

237
00:15:55,961 --> 00:15:59,720
Mechanics were often a big part of the entertainment.

238
00:15:59,721 --> 00:16:03,720
This is a Seberg Nickelodeon from the early 1900s.

239
00:16:03,721 --> 00:16:07,720
I'll open it up so you can see what's inside.

240
00:16:07,721 --> 00:16:10,720
When you put your quarter in, it drops into a slot

241
00:16:10,721 --> 00:16:12,720
which operates an electrical switch

242
00:16:12,721 --> 00:16:14,720
which sends a signal to the electric motor,

243
00:16:14,721 --> 00:16:18,720
and all of its function came from the paper roll.

244
00:16:18,721 --> 00:16:22,720
In this particular instrument, we have piano,

245
00:16:22,721 --> 00:16:25,720
and we have a set of violin pipes,

246
00:16:25,721 --> 00:16:27,720
and we also have what's called a mandolin rail,

247
00:16:27,721 --> 00:16:31,180
a mandolin rail, which makes the piano sound like a honky-tonk piano.

248
00:16:32,520 --> 00:16:37,580
When the tune is completed, this perforation crosses the tracker bar, sends a pneumatic

249
00:16:37,581 --> 00:16:41,480
signal to the electrical switch, and drops the coin into the coin box.

250
00:16:45,940 --> 00:16:51,500
Hey, phonographs became popular in the early 1900s and were the precursors to the jukebox,

251
00:16:51,501 --> 00:16:53,500
which first appeared in the 1920s.

252
00:16:53,501 --> 00:17:00,400
Companies like Rockola are still making jukeboxes, but with a 21st century flair.

253
00:17:01,660 --> 00:17:06,740
The first jukeboxes played the very large 78 RPM records, just one side.

254
00:17:07,600 --> 00:17:13,260
And of course that transcended into the 45 RPM record with a big hole in it in about 1949.

255
00:17:13,920 --> 00:17:16,800
And then eventually the CDs came in in the mid 80s.

256
00:17:16,801 --> 00:17:23,160
But this is transited from CDs over to a jukebox that is connected to the Internet,

257
00:17:24,160 --> 00:17:26,500
literally plays hundreds of thousands of CDs.

258
00:17:27,140 --> 00:17:28,000
This is a touchscreen.

259
00:17:28,740 --> 00:17:30,940
You can just go on it, touch what you would like,

260
00:17:31,380 --> 00:17:35,540
and it blasted out of this jukebox with 900 watts of power.

261
00:17:36,700 --> 00:17:38,760
Jukeboxes have managed to keep up with the times.

262
00:17:39,260 --> 00:17:43,780
But more coins are inserted into another part of the amusement business, gaming.

263
00:17:43,781 --> 00:17:50,000
In fact, at its peak, customers spent over $8 billion a year, mainly due to the lure of

264
00:17:50,001 --> 00:17:51,080
video games.

265
00:17:51,081 --> 00:17:57,340
This is an example of one of the first successful coin-operated Pong-style video games.

266
00:17:57,341 --> 00:18:02,800
It's very primitive by today's standards, but by 1972 standards, this was ahead of its

267
00:18:02,801 --> 00:18:03,800
time.

268
00:18:03,801 --> 00:18:06,540
Everyone was very impressed to be able to play a game on a TV set instead of just watching

269
00:18:06,541 --> 00:18:07,540
it.

270
00:18:07,541 --> 00:18:12,100
And then the microprocessor made the game ten times more complex and amazing.

271
00:18:12,101 --> 00:18:15,040
with the advent of games like Pac-Man a few years later,

272
00:18:15,041 --> 00:18:17,340
the public got really excited.

273
00:18:17,341 --> 00:18:18,940
Some players were even more excited

274
00:18:18,941 --> 00:18:20,980
when they found out they could fool the simple coin

275
00:18:20,981 --> 00:18:23,640
mechanisms used back then.

276
00:18:23,641 --> 00:18:27,220
In the late 70s and early 80s when video games came out,

277
00:18:27,221 --> 00:18:30,420
people became so creative they would go and drill holes

278
00:18:30,421 --> 00:18:33,180
through their quarters, tie a little bit of fishing line

279
00:18:33,181 --> 00:18:35,560
in there, put it in until it hit the micro switch,

280
00:18:35,561 --> 00:18:38,760
pull it back out, get their quarter, get a free game.

281
00:18:38,761 --> 00:18:41,960
Eventually the industry wised up and they put cutters in there,

282
00:18:41,961 --> 00:18:44,520
where as soon as you tried to pull anything back out,

283
00:18:44,521 --> 00:18:47,400
it would cut that line, quarter would go into the box,

284
00:18:47,401 --> 00:18:48,680
you'd still get your game,

285
00:18:48,681 --> 00:18:50,720
but the operator would still get his money.

286
00:18:53,040 --> 00:18:55,640
Before video games, pinball was king.

287
00:19:00,240 --> 00:19:03,680
In 1932, there were around 150 pinball machine

288
00:19:03,681 --> 00:19:05,420
manufacturers in the US.

289
00:19:06,700 --> 00:19:09,360
But now, only one survives.

290
00:19:09,361 --> 00:19:11,180
Stern Pinball.

291
00:19:11,181 --> 00:19:16,820
Located in Melrose Park, Illinois, the company's president, Gary Stern, has been around pinball

292
00:19:16,821 --> 00:19:19,200
most of his life.

293
00:19:19,201 --> 00:19:22,880
And the games his company now produces are much different than the ones his father used

294
00:19:22,881 --> 00:19:26,460
to sell.

295
00:19:26,461 --> 00:19:30,580
This is a pinball machine from the late 60s.

296
00:19:30,581 --> 00:19:35,020
These days they were much simpler games than we make today with electromechanical logic,

297
00:19:35,021 --> 00:19:38,460
relay step-up units, much like in an old telephone system.

298
00:19:38,461 --> 00:19:40,800
You can't make something this simple today.

299
00:19:40,920 --> 00:19:43,840
People are looking for more entertainment, more to do,

300
00:19:44,120 --> 00:19:46,940
much like you can't make cars with roll-up windows.

301
00:19:47,060 --> 00:19:48,420
People want power windows today.

302
00:19:48,760 --> 00:19:51,960
So now we make games with a lot of toys to play.

303
00:19:53,400 --> 00:19:55,060
They're much more complex.

304
00:19:55,680 --> 00:19:59,060
They do a lot more, and a lot more entertainment value.

305
00:20:00,540 --> 00:20:03,340
Stern Pinball produces three or four titles a year.

306
00:20:05,020 --> 00:20:07,260
Like this new Indiana Jones game.

307
00:20:08,460 --> 00:20:12,780
Stern's game designer first rendered it into 2D computer graphics.

308
00:20:12,781 --> 00:20:17,600
Then 3D computer artists created virtual versions of the working parts.

309
00:20:17,601 --> 00:20:21,760
Next Stern built a prototype play field

310
00:20:21,761 --> 00:20:25,900
to test the electronics and basic gameplay. Once approved

311
00:20:25,901 --> 00:20:29,820
the game went into production. This is the cable department

312
00:20:29,821 --> 00:20:33,580
we use an average of over a half a mile of wire in every pinball machine we

313
00:20:33,581 --> 00:20:34,560
manufacture

314
00:20:34,561 --> 00:20:37,360
We have operators that are on the line over here.

315
00:20:37,720 --> 00:20:42,060
It's a progressive line, and each operator will add one wire at a time

316
00:20:42,061 --> 00:20:44,120
until a complete wire harness is built.

317
00:20:45,480 --> 00:20:48,260
The wire harnesses travel down assembly lines

318
00:20:48,261 --> 00:20:51,820
as workers carefully craft an incredibly complex maze

319
00:20:51,821 --> 00:20:55,220
that includes all the electronic components and lights.

320
00:20:56,680 --> 00:21:00,780
On the top surface of the playfield, workers attach all of the hardware,

321
00:21:01,380 --> 00:21:04,960
including the flippers, bumpers, and the custom-designed props for each game.

322
00:21:06,120 --> 00:21:12,020
By the time they're done, each of Stern's games contains an average of 3,500 parts.

323
00:21:13,000 --> 00:21:14,480
We're in the play field test area.

324
00:21:14,700 --> 00:21:18,740
In this section over here, we test to make sure electrically and mechanically

325
00:21:18,741 --> 00:21:20,720
all the components on a play field are working.

326
00:21:21,160 --> 00:21:22,580
You have your flippers down here.

327
00:21:23,020 --> 00:21:24,320
You have your ball kickers.

328
00:21:24,860 --> 00:21:26,800
Shoot the ball back out onto a play field.

329
00:21:27,280 --> 00:21:29,480
The other thing, you have easy access to all the components

330
00:21:29,481 --> 00:21:30,780
so you could troubleshoot quicker.

331
00:21:31,280 --> 00:21:32,720
You could flip the playfield around.

332
00:21:33,120 --> 00:21:35,080
You have complete access to all your electrical

333
00:21:35,081 --> 00:21:36,440
and mechanical components,

334
00:21:36,720 --> 00:21:38,140
and that's how we troubleshoot them.

335
00:21:39,280 --> 00:21:41,200
If everything is working properly,

336
00:21:42,020 --> 00:21:44,520
workers install the completed playfields in the cabinets,

337
00:21:46,420 --> 00:21:47,900
fully test the finished games,

338
00:21:48,380 --> 00:21:50,380
and then prepare them for shipment.

339
00:21:51,260 --> 00:21:53,200
These games will ship throughout the world.

340
00:21:53,201 --> 00:21:55,840
We export about 40% of our games.

341
00:21:56,220 --> 00:21:58,260
Today we're making Austrian games,

342
00:21:58,261 --> 00:22:01,260
so they have to be set up for Austrian coinage,

343
00:22:01,360 --> 00:22:04,540
actually Euros, with German language and what have you.

344
00:22:05,860 --> 00:22:08,660
While pinball games have changed a lot over the years,

345
00:22:08,820 --> 00:22:10,620
there's one key element that hasn't.

346
00:22:11,700 --> 00:22:12,680
The pinball.

347
00:22:13,680 --> 00:22:16,420
It's 1 1⁄16 inches in diameter,

348
00:22:16,920 --> 00:22:19,720
weighs 0.17 pounds.

349
00:22:20,240 --> 00:22:21,740
It's going to move along the play field

350
00:22:21,741 --> 00:22:23,580
at 100 inches per minute.

351
00:22:24,240 --> 00:22:26,080
These were originally just ball bearings.

352
00:22:26,081 --> 00:22:28,360
I don't know if they're used for anything else now.

353
00:22:28,820 --> 00:22:31,580
I do know that our manufacturer has moved offshore,

354
00:22:32,080 --> 00:22:34,800
and we had a little problem getting these for a while,

355
00:22:34,880 --> 00:22:37,020
which would have been the end of the pinball business.

356
00:22:38,280 --> 00:22:42,380
For decades, making a gleaming pinball roll around and rack up points

357
00:22:42,381 --> 00:22:44,640
has endlessly fascinated some gamers.

358
00:22:46,560 --> 00:22:50,280
But other arcade machines turn your shiny coins into the game piece.

359
00:22:53,020 --> 00:22:54,640
When inserted into the machine,

360
00:22:54,641 --> 00:22:57,180
they don't fall directly into the coin box,

361
00:22:57,181 --> 00:22:59,020
but become part of the play,

362
00:22:59,021 --> 00:23:03,140
adding a little edge to winning or losing.

363
00:23:03,141 --> 00:23:04,680
When customers insert their coins

364
00:23:04,681 --> 00:23:06,760
into a certain variety of machine,

365
00:23:06,761 --> 00:23:11,160
they hope they'll cause other coins to fall off shelves.

366
00:23:11,161 --> 00:23:13,000
But all those coins teetering on the edge

367
00:23:13,001 --> 00:23:16,420
tempt some into not playing fair.

368
00:23:16,421 --> 00:23:18,000
Now, you get a lot of people who are always trying

369
00:23:18,001 --> 00:23:19,240
to cheat the arcade and the game.

370
00:23:19,241 --> 00:23:20,960
They think, give it a good swift shake,

371
00:23:20,961 --> 00:23:22,300
and you'll win some of those.

372
00:23:22,301 --> 00:23:23,800
But as you see, you shake it,

373
00:23:23,801 --> 00:23:26,440
the alarm goes off, those quarters fall down,

374
00:23:26,540 --> 00:23:28,940
but you don't get any tickets.

375
00:23:29,040 --> 00:23:32,940
In arcades, you win tickets instead of coins.

376
00:23:33,040 --> 00:23:34,040
No gambling allowed.

377
00:23:37,480 --> 00:23:39,740
Of course, countless fortunes have been deposited

378
00:23:39,840 --> 00:23:42,560
into casino slot machines by those hoping

379
00:23:42,660 --> 00:23:44,960
to turn a small investment into big winnings.

380
00:23:48,300 --> 00:23:50,460
But for some reason in an arcade,

381
00:23:50,560 --> 00:23:53,300
inserting way too many quarters to pluck a puny prize

382
00:23:53,301 --> 00:23:56,380
out of one of those claw machines is considered a victory.

383
00:23:58,260 --> 00:24:02,600
Before legislation, operators were able to change the tension on the claws

384
00:24:02,601 --> 00:24:06,580
or program the machines to allow a win every certain number of plays.

385
00:24:07,780 --> 00:24:11,520
Now everybody must get an equal, if still slim, chance of winning.

386
00:24:12,680 --> 00:24:18,040
When OK Manufacturing, the Gumball Guys, recently decided to manufacture a crane game,

387
00:24:18,680 --> 00:24:22,640
they came up with a kinder, gentler variation called tractor time.

388
00:24:23,300 --> 00:24:28,440
If you don't win a prize, it will keep going.

389
00:24:29,540 --> 00:24:32,940
It senses there's no prize, and in our machines,

390
00:24:33,040 --> 00:24:35,840
because they're winner every time, you get a second chance.

391
00:24:40,060 --> 00:24:44,140
A sensor and an eye will see the product pass through.

392
00:24:45,940 --> 00:24:48,460
Once the eye sees the product pass through here,

393
00:24:48,580 --> 00:24:50,880
it activates and tells the machine to stop.

394
00:24:50,881 --> 00:24:56,380
While arcades may be the most frivolous and fun way to use up pocket change,

395
00:24:56,381 --> 00:25:00,740
there are other coin-operated machines that perform important services.

396
00:25:02,100 --> 00:25:05,120
For nearly a century, we depended on one so much,

397
00:25:05,660 --> 00:25:09,300
we didn't dare leave home without a few coins, just in case.

398
00:25:10,580 --> 00:25:15,260
In 1988, the number of new pinball machines manufactured in the United States

399
00:25:15,261 --> 00:25:17,240
was about 100,000 units.

400
00:25:18,220 --> 00:25:22,780
Today, the yearly average of total games made is only about 10,000 units.

401
00:25:23,620 --> 00:25:26,080
Coin Operated will return on Modern Marvels.

402
00:25:34,800 --> 00:25:37,800
There's a blue-collar side to Coin Operated machines.

403
00:25:38,500 --> 00:25:40,340
They provide all kinds of services.

404
00:25:40,341 --> 00:25:47,540
from lockers and luggage cart rentals to laundromats.

405
00:25:47,940 --> 00:25:52,980
Today, we have about 35,000 laundromats in the United States.

406
00:25:53,420 --> 00:25:56,880
That's a $5 billion industry in gross sales.

407
00:25:57,560 --> 00:25:59,280
You know, everybody needs to wash their clothes.

408
00:26:01,260 --> 00:26:03,560
Coin-operated machines are also available

409
00:26:03,561 --> 00:26:05,460
to help clean out and wash our cars.

410
00:26:07,360 --> 00:26:09,440
But then again, it's because of our cars

411
00:26:09,441 --> 00:26:16,020
that we had to start paying for air, and also must deal with many people's least favorite

412
00:26:16,021 --> 00:26:17,120
coin-operative machine.

413
00:26:20,080 --> 00:26:24,140
There are currently over 5 million parking meters in the United States.

414
00:26:24,660 --> 00:26:29,340
The first one was installed in Oklahoma City on July 16, 1935.

415
00:26:30,120 --> 00:26:32,520
For the first 50 years, they were all mechanical.

416
00:26:33,520 --> 00:26:38,280
What we have here is a parking meter that was made in the 1950s.

417
00:26:38,281 --> 00:26:39,420
This is a parco meter.

418
00:26:40,040 --> 00:26:42,780
This has a big main spring in the meter.

419
00:26:43,420 --> 00:26:45,400
The technician would come by weekly

420
00:26:45,401 --> 00:26:48,100
and put the crank inside the back of the meter

421
00:26:48,101 --> 00:26:49,360
and would wind the timer,

422
00:26:49,600 --> 00:26:51,940
and they would store energy for the remainder of the week.

423
00:26:53,320 --> 00:26:55,980
Other mechanical meters let the customer do the winding

424
00:26:55,981 --> 00:26:57,560
after inserting each coin.

425
00:26:59,020 --> 00:27:01,960
Then in the mid-'80s, there was a new meter on the block.

426
00:27:02,900 --> 00:27:05,080
In 1985, the city of Beverly Hills

427
00:27:05,081 --> 00:27:07,080
began to replace their mechanical meters

428
00:27:07,081 --> 00:27:09,760
with this first-generation electronic meter citywide.

429
00:27:10,020 --> 00:27:13,140
The benefits are the single-coin slot for all of the coins,

430
00:27:14,480 --> 00:27:15,500
less-moving parts,

431
00:27:15,880 --> 00:27:18,220
and an electronic log of the valid and invalid coins

432
00:27:18,221 --> 00:27:19,220
that are inserted in the meter.

433
00:27:19,440 --> 00:27:21,780
Because it's electronic, it does take a 9-volt battery,

434
00:27:21,900 --> 00:27:23,340
which we replace about once a year.

435
00:27:24,940 --> 00:27:26,380
Beverly Hills, California,

436
00:27:26,381 --> 00:27:29,420
currently has over 3,100 meters in operation,

437
00:27:29,860 --> 00:27:33,340
and they bring in about $2.5 million a year.

438
00:27:33,341 --> 00:27:35,840
Will that be cash or credit?

439
00:27:35,841 --> 00:27:38,840
The city of Beverly Hills was the first city in the United

440
00:27:38,841 --> 00:27:40,840
States to deploy a credit card meter on the street.

441
00:27:40,841 --> 00:27:43,840
It still accepts coin, but you can insert your credit card.

442
00:27:43,841 --> 00:27:46,840
You can add more or less time.

443
00:27:46,841 --> 00:27:48,840
Press OK, and the meter will process your credit card

444
00:27:48,841 --> 00:27:49,840
immediately.

445
00:27:49,841 --> 00:27:53,840
It also has a solar panel, and it's capable of text messaging,

446
00:27:53,841 --> 00:27:55,840
our enforcement, and our maintenance personnel if the

447
00:27:55,841 --> 00:27:57,840
meter is vandalized or jammed.

448
00:27:57,841 --> 00:28:00,340
Well, I'm not sure if you're going to get a little bit

449
00:28:00,341 --> 00:28:01,041
jammed.

450
00:28:01,042 --> 00:28:09,080
While parking meters are surviving and thriving in the 21st century, payphones are

451
00:28:09,081 --> 00:28:14,912
barely hanging on. But there was a time when the payphone was indispensable.

452
00:28:14,913 --> 00:28:16,600
In 1964, when the U.S.

453
00:28:16,601 --> 00:28:19,709
Mint was ready to change the metallic composition of U.S.

454
00:28:19,710 --> 00:28:22,000
coins, they consulted with the Bell system

455
00:28:22,001 --> 00:28:27,280
and other interested parties to ensure that the new coins would still work in the public payphone

456
00:28:27,281 --> 00:28:28,661
system of the U.S.

457
00:28:28,662 --> 00:28:34,720
The first payphone, called a coin phone back then, went into service in Chicago

458
00:28:34,721 --> 00:28:40,171
in 1898. By 1902, there were over 80,000 in the United States.

459
00:28:40,172 --> 00:28:43,160
In 1910, there was even a portable

460
00:28:43,161 --> 00:28:43,861
model.

461
00:28:43,862 --> 00:28:49,800
This is an example of a three-slot payphone that is not mounted on a wall or found in a phone

462
00:28:49,801 --> 00:28:53,458
booth. It has a handle. It's made out of aluminum. It's fairly lightweight.

463
00:28:53,459 --> 00:28:54,580
It could be carried all

464
00:28:54,581 --> 00:28:57,480
over the Wanamaker department store for whom it was made.

465
00:28:57,660 --> 00:29:00,420
It was made so that the store employees could carry it

466
00:29:00,421 --> 00:29:02,640
into the restaurant or to other parts of the store

467
00:29:02,641 --> 00:29:03,700
for customer use.

468
00:29:04,040 --> 00:29:05,900
It had a long cord that could be plugged in

469
00:29:05,901 --> 00:29:07,100
wherever there was a phone jack.

470
00:29:08,160 --> 00:29:11,220
In the 1920s and 30s, special devices

471
00:29:11,221 --> 00:29:14,460
turned the regular phones of small businesses into pay phones.

472
00:29:15,100 --> 00:29:17,440
If I take the receiver off the hook

473
00:29:17,441 --> 00:29:19,080
in preparation for using the phone

474
00:29:19,081 --> 00:29:22,180
and drop a nickel in the slot and press the lever,

475
00:29:22,181 --> 00:29:25,960
it pulls the little arm or fork out of the dial,

476
00:29:26,280 --> 00:29:29,140
leaving the dial free to turn and for me to dial my call.

477
00:29:29,260 --> 00:29:30,340
When I'm finished with the call,

478
00:29:30,620 --> 00:29:32,060
I place the receiver on the hook,

479
00:29:32,400 --> 00:29:36,400
and the arm returns to the lock position in the dial.

480
00:29:36,700 --> 00:29:39,360
And it's ready for the next person to come along and make a call.

481
00:29:40,700 --> 00:29:43,180
The most surprising thing about commercial payphones

482
00:29:43,181 --> 00:29:46,980
is how simple the coin mechanisms were all the way up to 1965,

483
00:29:47,520 --> 00:29:49,740
before electronics were first incorporated.

484
00:29:52,180 --> 00:29:57,180
Here's a good example of a three-slot pay phone from the 50s.

485
00:29:57,181 --> 00:30:00,400
A local call would cost a dime or a nickel.

486
00:30:00,401 --> 00:30:03,500
If you wanted to make a long-distance call, you had to get the operator involved.

487
00:30:03,501 --> 00:30:06,260
That'll be 35 cents.

488
00:30:06,261 --> 00:30:11,480
And by getting the operator involved, she would be listening to a series of sounds as

489
00:30:11,481 --> 00:30:15,580
you put the coins through.

490
00:30:15,581 --> 00:30:20,300
In order for her to put the call through, she would listen, and they made distinctive

491
00:30:20,301 --> 00:30:21,300
sounds.

492
00:30:21,301 --> 00:30:25,540
the slot and go right to a bong sound.

493
00:30:25,541 --> 00:30:27,840
And she'd know that you'd put a quarter in.

494
00:30:27,841 --> 00:30:29,600
Next would be the dime.

495
00:30:29,601 --> 00:30:33,540
She would listen for two ding sounds.

496
00:30:33,541 --> 00:30:37,680
So that actually came around and hit the top of the bell and the bottom of the bell.

497
00:30:37,681 --> 00:30:39,360
Remarkably simple.

498
00:30:39,361 --> 00:30:43,520
Then you put the nickel in and it'll hit the bell one time.

499
00:30:43,521 --> 00:30:46,880
The phone had no idea of knowing how much money was collected.

500
00:30:46,881 --> 00:30:50,680
It was up to the operator and she kept count at the central office.

501
00:30:50,681 --> 00:30:55,120
and then she would go ahead and put your call through.

502
00:30:55,121 --> 00:30:58,080
In the 1990s, the number of payphones in the US

503
00:30:58,081 --> 00:31:01,800
reached over 2.6 million.

504
00:31:01,801 --> 00:31:04,040
But because of the explosion of cell phone usage

505
00:31:04,041 --> 00:31:07,320
over the last decade, the payphone is fading fast.

506
00:31:10,080 --> 00:31:12,480
Making sure you have some change before you leave the house

507
00:31:12,481 --> 00:31:15,000
isn't as important as it once was.

508
00:31:15,001 --> 00:31:17,040
So what can you do with all those extra coins?

509
00:31:17,041 --> 00:31:23,320
Turn them back into folding cash in another coin-op machine.

510
00:31:25,000 --> 00:31:29,260
In the 1950s, when aluminum and glass foam boots replaced wooden ones,

511
00:31:29,960 --> 00:31:33,980
several installed by the U.S. National Park Service were mysteriously vandalized.

512
00:31:33,981 --> 00:31:34,863
The culprits?

513
00:31:34,864 --> 00:31:40,160
Territorial male moose that charged the boots when they saw their reflections.

514
00:31:41,460 --> 00:31:44,140
Coin Operator will return on Modern Marvels.

515
00:31:47,040 --> 00:31:51,960
Many coin-operated machines are becoming less reliant on coins

516
00:31:51,961 --> 00:31:54,480
and more on alternative payment methods.

517
00:31:55,180 --> 00:31:56,560
Bill acceptors were only the beginning.

518
00:31:57,800 --> 00:32:01,380
Now many apartment tenants can simply swipe a smart card to do their laundry.

519
00:32:02,500 --> 00:32:06,300
And vending machines selling high-priced items may only take credit cards.

520
00:32:07,660 --> 00:32:11,660
Here at Macy's, we found that our customers really enjoy getting their gadgets from the vending machine.

521
00:32:11,661 --> 00:32:14,920
It's really quick and easy, and you can buy it within a matter of seconds.

522
00:32:14,921 --> 00:32:18,920
Change is becoming more of a leftover than a necessity.

523
00:32:18,921 --> 00:32:22,227
In fact, it's estimated that the average U.S.

524
00:32:22,228 --> 00:32:25,680
household has about $90 of spare change sitting

525
00:32:25,681 --> 00:32:27,680
around.

526
00:32:27,681 --> 00:32:32,220
Nationwide, that's about $10 billion worth.

527
00:32:32,221 --> 00:32:36,980
The CoinStar company found a way to cash in on the public's coin reserves by placing

528
00:32:36,981 --> 00:32:43,900
its high-speed counting machines in supermarkets and other retail outlets.

529
00:32:43,901 --> 00:32:51,500
In the 17 years Coinstar has been in business, it has processed over 350 billion coins.

530
00:32:51,501 --> 00:32:54,820
Right from the beginning of Coinstar, we networked all of our machines.

531
00:32:54,821 --> 00:32:59,400
So we know all the information from every single machine right across the world.

532
00:32:59,401 --> 00:33:04,980
Our average transaction was about $38, and our largest ever transaction was $13,000 in

533
00:33:04,981 --> 00:33:09,400
pennies from a gentleman in Alabama.

534
00:33:09,401 --> 00:33:13,860
Coinstar's main manufacturing facility is located in Kent, Washington.

535
00:33:13,861 --> 00:33:17,860
The workers assemble about a thousand machines a year here.

536
00:33:17,861 --> 00:33:19,360
The CoinStar machine is very simple to use.

537
00:33:19,361 --> 00:33:21,860
You bring your coin jar to the machine,

538
00:33:21,861 --> 00:33:25,860
follow the prompts, choose your language,

539
00:33:25,861 --> 00:33:29,860
and the coin machine asks you to put your coins directly into the machine.

540
00:33:29,861 --> 00:33:34,860
This is the first stage of actually cleaning the coins.

541
00:33:34,861 --> 00:33:37,860
As the consumers bring these in, you'd be shocked at what we see in here.

542
00:33:37,861 --> 00:33:42,860
We see paper clips, washers, we see all kinds of gum, everything else.

543
00:33:42,861 --> 00:33:46,860
notice that there are holes in here that are smaller than any of the coins.

544
00:33:46,861 --> 00:33:53,860
Then it goes through this piece where you have a turning mechanism here that actually is beating the coins apart.

545
00:33:53,861 --> 00:33:57,860
And as they beat the coins apart, also any debris that will fall out of there.

546
00:33:59,860 --> 00:34:01,860
I'm now going to open up the machine.

547
00:34:01,861 --> 00:34:04,860
And once all the debris has been removed through the cleaning process,

548
00:34:04,861 --> 00:34:08,860
all of the coins drop down into the coin counting device at the bottom here.

549
00:34:08,861 --> 00:34:12,020
This is our coin counting device outside of a machine.

550
00:34:12,760 --> 00:34:19,240
Over here behind this fancy black box is our intellectual property that we use to actually run this device.

551
00:34:20,180 --> 00:34:22,800
A consumer comes up, they drop their coins in.

552
00:34:23,520 --> 00:34:27,460
As it goes through here, you'll notice that this machine starts running through here.

553
00:34:28,020 --> 00:34:32,360
Each one of these arms are picking up coins and running it down this coin rail.

554
00:34:33,520 --> 00:34:36,700
As it runs through the coin rail, notice how the coins are separating.

555
00:34:36,701 --> 00:34:39,980
This allows time for each coin to be sensed.

556
00:34:40,400 --> 00:34:43,440
This sensor knows that it's a penny, a nickel, a dime,

557
00:34:43,580 --> 00:34:44,860
quarter, half dollar, dollar,

558
00:34:45,200 --> 00:34:48,440
and actually will recognize that and keep track of that in our system.

559
00:34:49,820 --> 00:34:52,140
Just past the coin sensor is a metal plate,

560
00:34:52,580 --> 00:34:54,640
moving too fast to be seen by the naked eye.

561
00:34:56,580 --> 00:34:58,500
When the sensor detects a good coin,

562
00:34:59,120 --> 00:35:01,280
the plate jumps forward and sends it down a chute,

563
00:35:01,580 --> 00:35:02,900
leading to the holding bins below.

564
00:35:02,901 --> 00:35:08,900
The plate doesn't jump when foreign coins and non-valid objects like slugs are sensed.

565
00:35:08,901 --> 00:35:12,900
And they fall down another chute so customers can recover their rejects.

566
00:35:12,901 --> 00:35:17,900
At the end of every transaction a voucher is produced, a fee is charged for using the service,

567
00:35:17,901 --> 00:35:20,900
and you then take the voucher to the checkout to exchange for cash.

568
00:35:20,901 --> 00:35:23,900
Or you can choose a gift card now and get free coin counting.

569
00:35:23,901 --> 00:35:28,900
The counted coins are held inside the machine in two secure steel bins.

570
00:35:29,580 --> 00:35:31,960
Each bin holds over 500 pounds of coin.

571
00:35:32,200 --> 00:35:34,360
Our network tells us when to collect these bins.

572
00:35:34,780 --> 00:35:38,700
We have special moving devices that allow our companies to come in and collect these coins

573
00:35:38,701 --> 00:35:41,200
and take them directly to the processing centers for counting.

574
00:35:42,120 --> 00:35:48,320
In the Los Angeles area, trucks bring CoinStar's bins to a coin processing center operated by Brinks.

575
00:35:50,680 --> 00:35:54,160
The driver rolls the bins off the truck into a secure loading dock,

576
00:35:54,520 --> 00:35:58,060
in which the inner and outer doors are never open at the same time.

577
00:35:58,900 --> 00:36:05,460
Once all the full bins are exchanged for empty ones, the inner door opens and the full bins

578
00:36:05,461 --> 00:36:08,380
are rolled into the facility.

579
00:36:08,381 --> 00:36:13,340
Brink's employees place each bin on an elevator and then unlock it so the coins can pour into

580
00:36:13,341 --> 00:36:16,420
a sorting device.

581
00:36:16,421 --> 00:36:22,660
Vibrating disks full of holes eliminate anything bigger or smaller than U.S. coins.

582
00:36:22,661 --> 00:36:27,180
Then they travel up a conveyor and into a counting and sorting mechanism.

583
00:36:27,181 --> 00:36:30,020
These machines here have the capability of processing

584
00:36:30,021 --> 00:36:34,020
approximately 5,000 to 8,000 coins per minute.

585
00:36:34,021 --> 00:36:36,620
Not only is it verifying and counting the coin,

586
00:36:36,621 --> 00:36:41,540
but also it's separating the coin by denomination.

587
00:36:41,541 --> 00:36:45,580
Each denomination pours down a tube and into a bin.

588
00:36:45,581 --> 00:36:49,420
When a bin is full, it's moved over to a scale.

589
00:36:49,421 --> 00:36:52,620
They can wait to verify that the appropriate amount of money

590
00:36:52,621 --> 00:36:54,820
that was processed is actually in the bin.

591
00:36:57,180 --> 00:37:05,600
When it's time to prepare the coins for recirculation, workers dump them into another series of machines, each designed to handle a specific denomination.

592
00:37:07,880 --> 00:37:16,080
This machine is counting quarters, organizing them into a steady stream and inserting 40 quarters at a time into a plastic sleeve.

593
00:37:19,160 --> 00:37:26,960
Now human hands take over and place the plastic-wrapped tubes of coins into boxes and box after box onto pallets.

594
00:37:27,180 --> 00:37:29,680
This is the largest coin center in the world.

595
00:37:29,681 --> 00:37:33,420
We process over 3 billion coins just for coins to our launch.

596
00:37:33,421 --> 00:37:35,680
These boxes here are going back into circulation

597
00:37:35,681 --> 00:37:38,720
for our customers such as banks, supermarkets,

598
00:37:38,721 --> 00:37:41,720
and as well here we have canvas bags filled with coins

599
00:37:41,721 --> 00:37:43,720
that will be going to the Federal Reserve

600
00:37:43,721 --> 00:37:45,640
for recirculation.

601
00:37:45,641 --> 00:37:47,840
With all those billions of coins

602
00:37:47,841 --> 00:37:50,640
going back into circulation, a few of them

603
00:37:50,641 --> 00:37:52,640
will undoubtedly find their way.

604
00:37:57,180 --> 00:38:01,960
into some of the weirdest coin-operated machines ever devised.

605
00:38:05,380 --> 00:38:07,220
According to the U.S. Mint,

606
00:38:07,580 --> 00:38:11,260
the average life expectancy of a coin in circulation is 30 years,

607
00:38:11,940 --> 00:38:15,260
while paper money lasts only about 18 months.

608
00:38:16,540 --> 00:38:19,660
Coin Operated will return on Modern Marvels.

609
00:38:19,661 --> 00:38:32,780
Modern arcade games may offer wild interactivity and imaginative graphics, but some of the

610
00:38:32,781 --> 00:38:38,420
mechanical coin-operated amusements of the past delivered their own special brand of

611
00:38:38,421 --> 00:38:45,500
fascinating, eccentric, and often bizarre entertainment.

612
00:38:45,501 --> 00:38:51,240
At Musée Mechanique, located on Fisherman's Wharf in San Francisco, Dan Zielinski uses

613
00:38:51,241 --> 00:38:53,880
roller skates to race around a huge room.

614
00:38:55,120 --> 00:38:59,280
It houses the vast collection his late father began, for the public to enjoy.

615
00:39:02,240 --> 00:39:04,900
Most of the machines will set you back just a quarter or two.

616
00:39:05,640 --> 00:39:09,280
Of all the machines that my father and I have collected here at the Musée Mechanique,

617
00:39:09,281 --> 00:39:11,100
this is the largest one in the collection.

618
00:39:11,101 --> 00:39:15,000
It was made in the 1930s, and it was all hand carved

619
00:39:15,100 --> 00:39:17,440
by a single individual.

620
00:39:17,540 --> 00:39:20,140
This is a great deal for the public in general.

621
00:39:20,240 --> 00:39:23,740
For 50 cents, you can get 10, 20, or 30 people to crowd

622
00:39:23,840 --> 00:39:25,940
around this and watch the entire thing come to life

623
00:39:26,040 --> 00:39:27,140
at the drop of a coin.

624
00:39:30,560 --> 00:39:34,460
Usually, the appeal of the machines is obvious.

625
00:39:34,560 --> 00:39:38,460
Who doesn't enjoy an all-monkey band?

626
00:39:38,560 --> 00:39:40,100
Today, almost every vintage machine

627
00:39:40,101 --> 00:39:44,700
machine has some loyal fans.

628
00:39:44,701 --> 00:39:47,240
There are some machines that must have been a bit of a letdown

629
00:39:47,241 --> 00:39:49,900
when customers first had the chance to put their hard-earned

630
00:39:49,901 --> 00:39:53,600
coins in them.

631
00:39:53,601 --> 00:39:57,860
But other curiosities are well worth the investment.

632
00:39:57,861 --> 00:40:01,500
This circus is made entirely out of toothpicks.

633
00:40:01,501 --> 00:40:03,100
Obviously, this sort of intricate work

634
00:40:03,101 --> 00:40:07,460
is done by someone with a lot of spare time on their hands.

635
00:40:07,461 --> 00:40:09,940
This is a toothpick Ferris wheel.

636
00:40:09,941 --> 00:40:15,200
It's not terribly elaborate, but what makes it interesting is it was made by a San Quentin prisoner.

637
00:40:15,920 --> 00:40:21,500
It's part of a larger display that was constructed of over 150,000 toothpicks.

638
00:40:22,760 --> 00:40:26,400
The vintage oddities at the Musée Mécanique are certainly unique.

639
00:40:27,600 --> 00:40:34,700
But a search for truly weird coin-operated fare inevitably brings you to Farmington Hills, Michigan.

640
00:40:35,720 --> 00:40:39,800
Welcome to Marvin's, known from coast to coast like butter and toast.

641
00:40:39,940 --> 00:40:43,140
nowhere in the contiguous United States or other environs

642
00:40:43,141 --> 00:40:44,720
in a place like Marvin's.

643
00:40:45,220 --> 00:40:45,920
Welcome.

644
00:40:46,380 --> 00:40:47,080
Have fun.

645
00:40:48,380 --> 00:40:51,260
Just about every inch of ceiling, wall, and floor space

646
00:40:51,261 --> 00:40:52,360
is jam-packed.

647
00:40:53,220 --> 00:40:55,920
The museum is over 5,000 square feet

648
00:40:55,921 --> 00:40:58,560
and has over 1,000 electrical outlets

649
00:40:58,561 --> 00:41:01,320
to power all of the historic and modern machines.

650
00:41:04,000 --> 00:41:05,960
Marvin's interest in these mechanical wonders

651
00:41:05,961 --> 00:41:07,540
began about 50 years ago.

652
00:41:07,541 --> 00:41:12,680
When I was in college, I was fascinated by these coin-operated machines and I started

653
00:41:12,681 --> 00:41:13,680
collecting.

654
00:41:13,681 --> 00:41:18,480
Well, after I graduated in the early 60s, I couldn't quit collecting.

655
00:41:18,481 --> 00:41:22,700
Even though he's a pharmacist every day by trade, he started collecting and collecting

656
00:41:22,701 --> 00:41:23,700
this stuff.

657
00:41:23,701 --> 00:41:25,300
He overtook my mother's house.

658
00:41:25,301 --> 00:41:29,460
In 1980, she took me to a food court and thought, what a great place for your dad to get this

659
00:41:29,461 --> 00:41:37,060
stuff out of my house and put it for other people to enjoy.

660
00:41:37,061 --> 00:41:42,060
Marvin's still collecting.

661
00:41:42,061 --> 00:41:45,060
And whenever traveling abroad, he's always on the lookout for

662
00:41:45,061 --> 00:41:50,060
unusual coin-operated contraptions to add to his museum.

663
00:41:50,061 --> 00:41:53,060
Just recently, going to England, I found another breed of

664
00:41:53,061 --> 00:41:56,060
machines, these wooden automata that the craftsmen are still

665
00:41:56,061 --> 00:41:58,060
making these machines there.

666
00:41:58,061 --> 00:42:03,060
So there's a place to buy old ones and new ones, which I like

667
00:42:03,061 --> 00:42:06,060
equally well.

668
00:42:06,061 --> 00:42:10,820
Many of the old Penny Arcade machines have a decidedly dark bent.

669
00:42:10,821 --> 00:42:16,940
Intricate motors and gears cause small robotic figures to act out all sorts of ghoulish scenes,

670
00:42:16,941 --> 00:42:21,360
including the horrors of the Spanish Inquisition.

671
00:42:21,361 --> 00:42:25,940
In keeping with his grisly tradition, Marvin decided his customers might enjoy firing up

672
00:42:25,941 --> 00:42:26,940
an electric chair.

673
00:42:26,941 --> 00:42:35,500
Now this machine I found is a Halloween prop, and I added a case and a coin-accepted mechanism

674
00:42:35,501 --> 00:42:36,340
And watch.

675
00:42:49,680 --> 00:42:52,080
It may be a little too intense for younger children.

676
00:42:53,920 --> 00:42:56,960
Another machine turns the tables on Marvin's customers

677
00:42:56,961 --> 00:42:59,620
and delivers a shocking experience.

678
00:42:59,621 --> 00:43:01,900
This machine is Challenge Killer Watt,

679
00:43:01,901 --> 00:43:03,920
machine unique to my museum.

680
00:43:03,921 --> 00:43:08,160
It was built by a friend of mine, a robotics engineer, Ohio.

681
00:43:08,161 --> 00:43:12,680
And the idea of the machine is to keep your hands

682
00:43:12,681 --> 00:43:15,280
as long as you can on this handle.

683
00:43:15,281 --> 00:43:20,400
Oh, a cute little hero to take on killer one, huh?

684
00:43:20,401 --> 00:43:23,480
Actually, a little static electricity is produced.

685
00:43:23,481 --> 00:43:26,960
Very light and very non-dangerous.

686
00:43:26,961 --> 00:43:28,280
But it does give you a little tingle.

687
00:43:28,281 --> 00:43:29,280
Don't worry.

688
00:43:29,281 --> 00:43:31,680
Try a little of this.

689
00:43:31,681 --> 00:43:33,280
Whoa.

690
00:43:33,281 --> 00:43:34,180
Hey, lovely.

691
00:43:34,181 --> 00:43:36,220
In the old days, there were some machines

692
00:43:36,221 --> 00:43:37,480
that did give you a shock.

693
00:43:37,481 --> 00:43:42,420
But of course, in today's litigious society,

694
00:43:42,421 --> 00:43:44,920
we can't have anything like that.

695
00:43:44,921 --> 00:43:47,860
With the right mindset, it's easy to spend a small fortune

696
00:43:47,861 --> 00:43:50,040
in coins here.

697
00:43:50,041 --> 00:43:52,300
But we're going to drop our last couple of quarters

698
00:43:52,301 --> 00:43:55,140
into another one of Marvin's unique machines,

699
00:43:55,141 --> 00:43:59,980
Dr. Ralph Bingenpurge, a real showstopper.

700
00:44:03,280 --> 00:44:17,280
Like this.

